17:- Interfaces merge only when every declaration of the name is at module level (members by name, `extends` accumulated, the first description and the first tag values win). An interface declared in a function body or block is not isolated: it shares the module-level key, so a lone one is collected under its bare name (a factory's result can name it) and a later module-level one replaces it, but two local interfaces of one name in different scopes overwrite each other, and a local one never merges into or replaces a module-level one. TypeScript treats each scope's declaration as its own type. An interface declared directly in a `switch` case clause is treated as module-level, so it can merge with a module-level interface of the same name. Enums and classes are keyed by bare name the same way and are not isolated either.

